[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Footage from the Insta360 Luna Ultra, showcasing various cinematic shots.
AUDIO: 'Hey, I\'m Brandon Li, and this is going to&nbsp; be my first look at the Insta360 Luna Ultra. Okay, so this is the Luna Ultra. It\'s a gimbal&nbsp; stabilized camera. It is a bit bigger and heavier'

[00:33] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: A woman looking into the distance, filmed with the Insta360 Luna Ultra.
AUDIO: 'than previous pocket gimbal cameras because it&nbsp; has two lenses instead of one. These are Leica lenses with Leica Color Science. There\'s a 1×&nbsp; lens with an F1.8 aperture and a 1-inch sensor. And there\'s a 3× lens with an F2 aperture and&nbsp; a slightly smaller one over 1.3-inch sensor.'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And this 3× lens can also focus very close,&nbsp; allowing almost macro-level close-ups. You can zoom through the entire range, but there&nbsp; are some digital sensor crops, and there\'s a'

[01:03] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Split screen comparison of a building shot with the Insta360 Luna Ultra.
AUDIO: 'sudden switch when you go from the 1× lens&nbsp; to the 3× lens and then it digitally crops all the way up to 12×. But of course, there&nbsp; is some image quality loss at maximum zoom. The Luna Ultra shoots up to 8K with Dolby&nbsp; Vision support or 4K at 120 frames per second,'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which you\'re seeing here. It shoots 10-bit log&nbsp; in Insta360\'s own I-Log format, which is how I shot all the footage in this review, unless otherwise&nbsp; noted. I-Log supports the ACES professional'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'color grading workflow. Another feature worth&nbsp; mentioning is Focus Breathing Compensation, where the camera digitally crops in to avoid the slight&nbsp; zooming that you see as the focus adjusts itself. Notice how the background seems to zoom as I move&nbsp; with Focus Breathing Compensation, this goes away.'

[01:47] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Creator holding the Insta360 Luna Ultra, demonstrating its form factor and the small screen.
AUDIO: 'The other major new feature is the detachable&nbsp; touchscreen remote control. This touchscreen lets you trigger focus as well as tracking. It can&nbsp; track people or objects, and you can also turn the'

[02:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'power on and off, start and stop recording, and&nbsp; control the joystick and the zoom. And, you can use the remote as a microphone, or you can connect&nbsp; up to two Mic Pro microphones without the need for a separate receiver. And to top it all off, the&nbsp; Luna Ultra handle includes these tiny tripod feet,'

[02:18] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: On-screen text "Detachable Screen" appears, introducing a key feature.
AUDIO: 'so you don\'t need to use a separate mini tripod&nbsp; when you want to set the camera down. So, when you put all these features together, you get a great&nbsp; travel camera for doing cinematic gimbal shots at multiple focal lengths, plus easy vlogging&nbsp; and tracking. Okay, those are the basics. Now,'

[02:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m going to dive into my tips for getting the&nbsp; most out of the Luna Ultra. And by the way, almost all the footage in this video was shot&nbsp; on the Luna, including the behind-the-scenes. Let\'s kick things off by taking a little tram ride&nbsp; around town. One of the main new features of the'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Luna is this detachable screen. So, the screen of&nbsp; the Luna can actually just be removed like that, and then it becomes a remote control. So, I could&nbsp; face the camera one direction, and then I can look at this tiny screen in my hand and see exactly&nbsp; what I\'m shooting with wireless video. And also,'

[03:03] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Creator detaching the touchscreen remote from the Insta360 Luna Ultra.
AUDIO: 'I can use this as a microphone if I want to film&nbsp; myself and hold the camera somewhere else. Next, we\'re going to open up those little tripod feet&nbsp; and I\'m going to show you how to get a more'

[03:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'professional-looking vlog shot using the 3× lens.&nbsp; So, I\'ve just set up the Luna to film us at this restaurant. It\'s about 3 meters away with the 3× lens.&nbsp; So, that gives us this sort of telephoto look. So, this little touchcreen is how I adjusted the&nbsp; gimbal, adjusting the joystick. Pan it, tilt it,'

[03:30] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Close-up of the detached touchscreen remote, showing its USB-C port.
AUDIO: 'set up the shot however I want. And I\'m also using&nbsp; the microphone that\'s on top of this removable screen as my mic as I talk to you right now. And&nbsp; then to set my focus, I tap on myself on this'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'little screen. So, if I tap on the background,&nbsp; it\'s shifting the focus back there. And if I tap on me, it shifts the focus back to me. Now,&nbsp; I\'m going to set down this screen. So now it\'s still recording my voice. Right now I\'m about&nbsp; to eat a prawn dumpling with Yuzu sauce on it.'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you want to do vlogging shots on a&nbsp; tripod, the Luna Ultra makes it a lot easier with the detachable touchscreen remote.&nbsp; So the removable screen makes it a lot easier'

[04:10] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Creator using the detached remote to control the camera and track himself.
AUDIO: 'to set up a vlog of myself because I can&nbsp; preview exactly what I\'m shooting on the little screen right here. And I\'m also able&nbsp; to start tracking immediately just by single clicking on the joystick. So it just detected&nbsp; me as a subject. And now as I move around,'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it will track wherever I go. And if I\'m not&nbsp; satisfied with the framing of this tracking, I can just adjust the joystick accordingly. Like, let\'s say I want to show more of the church. Okay, now it will track with me at the bottom of the&nbsp; frame and the church above me as I move around.'

[04:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And this will also work with the 3× lens. So&nbsp; let\'s zoom in there. Okay, I zoomed in and now it is still tracking me as I move around. It\'s&nbsp; still able to keep me tracked, and I\'m still using'

[04:52] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Creator looking at the detached remote screen while the camera tracks him.
AUDIO: 'the microphone. Everything else still works the&nbsp; same. So, you have freedom to choose between your focal lengths as you track yourself. And you can&nbsp; zoom all the way into 12× if you want. Although, there is some loss of quality there, or just go&nbsp; back to the 1× lens. Zooming out. Zooming out.'

[05:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There we go. Now, there\'s one thing you should&nbsp; be aware of if you\'re tracking yourself with the Luna. If you go too far off to one side,&nbsp; you will hit a limit. You see that? That\'s the'

[05:19] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: The Insta360 Luna Ultra tracking the creator as he walks, demonstrating the gimbal's limit.
AUDIO: 'limit of the gimbal. So, it can\'t go any further&nbsp; and it\'s going to lose the tracking. Eventually, it will lose me. But if I go to the other side,&nbsp; there is no limit. It can just keep tracking and tracking and tracking and tracking&nbsp; and then I\'m fine. So, the thing to do here is to'

[05:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'angle your gimbal like that. Angle your Luna&nbsp; more like that. So that way you can walk in either direction a lot further without hitting the&nbsp; limit at all until I go behind the tree. Finally,'

[05:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here\'s some low-light footage shot on the&nbsp; Luna Ultra. You get good performance in low light with both lenses. Though the 1× lens has&nbsp; a slightly bigger sensor and therefore slightly'

[06:04] [scene:result]
VISUAL: Low-light footage shot with the Insta360 Luna Ultra.
AUDIO: 'better sensitivity. Insta360 has a special noise&nbsp; reduction mode called PureVideo which will give you a cleaner looking image. But this comes at&nbsp; the cost of some loss of detail. You can shoot in PureVideo up to 4K resolution and up to 60 frames&nbsp; per second. So basically, if you don\'t want to do'

[06:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'any post-processing, use PureVideo. If you plan&nbsp; on doing color grading and noise reduction, use the majority of people using the Luna&nbsp; will probably use it as a vlogging camera,'

[06:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so they\'ll just pull out the camera and start&nbsp; talking. So this is what it sounds like if you use the built-in audio. This is the audio straight&nbsp; out of the camera. I haven\'t applied any filters or anything to change it. I\'m in a busy urban&nbsp; area, so there\'s a lot of background noise'

[06:43] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Creator demonstrating the built-in audio pickup patterns of the camera.
AUDIO: 'around me. So, this is just the basic audio that&nbsp; you\'ll get from the Luna. However, the Luna will let you adjust the pickup pattern of the built-in&nbsp; mic. That means that I can choose to only record the front audio, my own voice. That\'s what you\'re&nbsp; hearing right now. And I can have it reject all of'

[06:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that audio out there. So, that makes it a lot&nbsp; easier to get clean audio of yourself without relying on digital noise reduction. And you can&nbsp; also have it record audio in front and behind the camera. This is not the same pickup pattern as&nbsp; omnidirectional because it\'s still rejecting audio'

[07:12] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Creator holding the Insta360 Luna Ultra, showing the screen and controls.
AUDIO: 'from the sides. So, if I\'m talking to the camera&nbsp; here and then I walk off to the side, you\'ll hear that my voice is not as loud anymore, and then it&nbsp; gets louder as I walk around to the back of the camera. And then it sounds the same when I\'m back&nbsp; here at the front. So, let\'s say I\'m holding the'

[07:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera like this, and I\'m interviewing somebody out&nbsp; there, but I still want to be able to hear myself talk from behind the camera just as clearly.&nbsp; That\'s what this pickup pattern is best for. If you want to be able to get more distance from&nbsp; the Luna with your audio, then you can use the Mic'

[07:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Pro, which is what you\'re hearing right now.&nbsp; It records internal 32-Bit Float stereo. So, basically what that means is that it records&nbsp; left and right channels. It\'s stereo. And it\'s 32-bit float, which means that it will never&nbsp; peak. The audio will never be too loud so that'

[07:57] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Creator demonstrating the Mic Pro for improved audio.
AUDIO: 'it\'s distorted. You can always bring it back&nbsp; in post. There\'s a new feature of the Luna, which may not seem like a big deal, but it is a&nbsp; big deal to me, which is the ability to lock the gimbal. So on previous pocket gimbal cameras, the&nbsp; gimbal would always move when you move your hand.'

[08:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So it will just constantly either pan or tilt.&nbsp; There\'s no way to completely lock it off. Or if you could lock it, you had to hold down a button.&nbsp; But with the Luna, I can set a custom button to press once, and it will stay locked. So you swipe&nbsp; down from the top, and then you swipe over to your'

[08:27] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: On-screen text "Fake Drone Shot" appears.
AUDIO: 'Custom Button settings. Click on that and then&nbsp; choose your right button. Choose single press and Lock/Unlock Gimbal Direction. So now&nbsp; when I click on the right button of the screen,'

[08:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the gimbal orientation is locked. The chicken&nbsp; head mode, it\'s not following the pan or the tilt. Let\'s talk about how to do a fake drone shot&nbsp; with the Luna because it\'s a little bit different than with other small gimbal cameras because of&nbsp; this removable screen. So the first thing I\'m'

[08:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to want to do is remove that screen. Then I&nbsp; will extend out the gimbal on the extension rod. I\'m using my joystick to adjust my framing a bit.&nbsp; And I\'m going to press my right custom button. And'

[09:10] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Creator demonstrating how to achieve a "fake drone shot" by locking the gimbal and using the detached remote.
AUDIO: 'I\'m locking the orientation of the gimbal with&nbsp; that button. So now my gimbal\'s locked, and no matter how I move, it will always face the same&nbsp; direction. It will always face forward. So now as'

[09:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m filming my shot, I don\'t have to actually look&nbsp; up at the Luna at all. I can just look at this little screen in front of me and the orientation&nbsp; is locked. So, I don\'t have to worry about the shot accidentally panning to the left, panning&nbsp; to the right, or tilting in a way that I don\'t'

[09:36] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Close-up of the Insta360 Luna Ultra's custom button being pressed to lock the gimbal.
AUDIO: 'want. And one more little feature I appreciate of&nbsp; the Luna is that I can use this handle as a quick release to quickly put the camera on a selfie&nbsp; stick or some kind of extension or tripod here and then quickly remove it. So, basically, I just&nbsp; leave the handle always connected to the extension'

[09:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rod, and then I remove the camera as necessary&nbsp; if I want to just go handheld for a while, and then drop it back on when I want to use the&nbsp; extension rod again. Here\'s a tip for how I like to get low-angle shots with the Luna. So, you&nbsp; could just try to hold it really low and shoot,'

[10:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which might work, but it\'s a little bit awkward.&nbsp; It\'s kind of hard to hold it like this at a really low angle. So, what I like to do is I just turn&nbsp; it upside down like this and then I shoot. Now, it\'s hard to see the screen when it\'s upside&nbsp; down like this. So, I remove it and then I'

[10:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hold the screen in my hand, and the screen will&nbsp; automatically rotate the image. So, you\'re seeing a right-side-up image, and then I just hold it&nbsp; down there. So, here I\'m holding the camera low and I\'m looking into the screen, and I can use&nbsp; my joystick to set the framing however I want.'

[10:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And once I\'m happy with the framing, I can press&nbsp; my custom right button and that locks it. And I\'m going to crouch down before I walk so I can get&nbsp; the camera as low as possible to the ground and'

[10:56] [scene:result]
VISUAL: Footage shot with the optional Wide-Angle Lens.
AUDIO: 'do my ninja walk really smooth. And then I\'ll be&nbsp; able to get a nice-looking low-angle shot with the Luna here. The Luna Ultra has an optional&nbsp; Wide-Angle Lens, which snaps on magnetically. This only affects your 1× lens, so it doesn\'t&nbsp; change the field of view of the 3×. It does'

[11:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'add fisheye distortion, but this can easily be&nbsp; corrected in post using a lens correction effect. You can also get neutral density filters,&nbsp; which also attach magnetically. ND filters,'

[11:27] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Creator holding the white version of the Insta360 Luna Ultra, demonstrating ND filters.
AUDIO: 'of course, are essential for getting&nbsp; proper motion blur and bright light, but you got to be very careful with these&nbsp; filters because it\'s really easy to drop them or get fingerprints on them. So far,&nbsp; my tactic has been to mostly just use the ND16 in the daytime to avoid switching them.&nbsp; I generally shoot at 48p. I either set the'

[11:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shutter speed manually to 1/100, or I put it&nbsp; on Auto and let it just self-adjust. Okay, that\'s my video about the Insta360 Luna Ultra. If&nbsp; you want to learn more about it, there will be a link in the description. Otherwise, please click&nbsp; like and subscribe, and I will see you next time.'
